WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.560
Part 3. You will hear two food science students called Nadia and Fergus talking to their tutor

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:16.879
about their course. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:52.080
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:01:01.200
Thanks for coming along, Nadia and Fergus. So this is a chance for you to give us some

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.239
feedback about different aspects of your course. What would you like to begin with?

00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:12.080
I've enjoyed the sessions on food safety. There was some information there that I found

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:18.160
really surprising, although some was stuff I knew already, like the rise in rates of obesity.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.839
Yeah, that's been in the news a lot. But I hadn't realised that unsafe food causes more than

00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:30.959
200 different diseases. No, I had no idea it was that many. And speaking of diseases,

00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:37.279
I knew resistance to antibiotics is on the increase, but I didn't know why.

00:01:37.279 --> 00:01:43.199
That it's partly because when animals are treated with antibiotics then consumed by humans,

00:01:43.199 --> 00:01:47.440
the antibiotics get into the food chain. I had no idea about that either.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.639
Then the sessions provided a lot of information about plastic pollution from food packaging in

00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:58.879
the ocean. But I think that most of us were already aware of that.

00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:03.599
Yeah, but I thought we could have done more on how much food is thrown away,

00:02:03.599 --> 00:02:08.399
unnecessarily through fear of it being out of date. That was only mentioned in passing.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:15.440
OK, I'll bear that in mind. What did you think about the sessions from visiting lecturers?

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:15.940
Fergus?

00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:20.800
For me, the most interesting one was about that project to prevent companies

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.320
giving incorrect information to consumers about food.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.119
Ah, food fraud, yes.

00:02:27.119 --> 00:02:31.600
I thought it was really good to address a problem that's faced by so many different groups.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.960
People with special religious rules, as well as vegetarians and vegans.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.720
And those with allergies.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.960
Yeah, and another thing. We've had effective ways of analysing DNA for some time now,

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.000
and these can easily be applied to analysing food. But what the researchers succeeded in

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.360
doing was to ensure that these tests were carried out at different stages in the food's journey,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.679
from the producer to the consumer.

00:02:57.679 --> 00:03:01.119
So they knew that the food actually came from the place it was supposed to,

00:03:01.679 --> 00:03:04.160
and had the ingredients it was meant to.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.160
Exactly. So customers can be confident about what they buy.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.039
And the researchers had a good system for publicising their findings, too.

00:03:13.039 --> 00:03:16.080
Well, I thought there were some problems with that, actually.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.960
OK. And do you have any recommendations for new topics that we could include in the course?

00:03:23.759 --> 00:03:29.440
Well, I'm interested in how crop yields can be increased without damaging the environment.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.479
But we've already done quite a bit on that, but not so much on the seafood industry,

00:03:34.479 --> 00:03:38.320
where stocks are in danger of being overexploited as a food source,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.279
unless we can find ways of keeping stocks up.

00:03:41.279 --> 00:03:48.240
Yes, that'd be a good topic. And I'm interested in the idea of a personalised approach to diet.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.880
Now we have the technology to analyse exactly what individuals need.

00:03:53.759 --> 00:03:57.440
That sounds more like a medical topic than food science.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:04.080
OK. What about sessions on the variety of food and eating habits around the world?

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.000
That's very relevant nowadays.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.360
Yes, I think the whole class would be interested in that.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.960
Then there's technological stuff, things like 3D printing of food and smart packaging.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.279
Maybe two specialists.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:26.079
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30.

00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:56.079
Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.

00:04:56.799 --> 00:05:00.239
Now, I'm particularly interested in your project,

00:05:00.239 --> 00:05:05.679
the one where you developed a new food product. So, talk me through the stages.

00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:09.760
First, you had to decide on your initial aim.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.760
We decided we wanted to create something people could eat on the go, rather than in a restaurant.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:21.200
Yeah, so we chose falafel, which was originally a Middle Eastern snack.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.079
We made up our minds about that pretty quickly.

00:05:24.079 --> 00:05:28.640
I know some students found this a lot harder to choose and wasted a lot of time.

00:05:29.279 --> 00:05:34.399
Then we had to do the literature review. We hadn't done one of these before,

00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:38.559
so the handout with advice for the project was very useful here.

00:05:38.559 --> 00:05:42.559
Yes, especially the advice on how to present the information.

00:05:43.519 --> 00:05:48.880
Then, product development, actually deciding what we'd use to make the falafel.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.679
And for me, the interesting thing about that

00:05:51.679 --> 00:05:56.320
was that we wanted it to be something a bit different from an ordinary falafel.

00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:01.200
We really made the right choice when we finally decided to use

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.399
jackfruit, even though it wasn't something that either of us had ever tasted before.

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:12.959
Yeah, like the name tells you, it's a fruit, but actually it's really good in savoury dishes.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.760
The product production, working out how to make the falafel,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.679
was harder than I expected because I'd never made them before.

00:06:21.679 --> 00:06:26.880
It was mostly trial and error. We started off with the basic recipe,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.239
and then experimented, and when it went wrong…

00:06:30.239 --> 00:06:32.160
Which it did a lot of the time.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.359
We just moved on and kept adapting it.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.839
And in the end, it turned out fine, and we had a lot of fun.

00:06:39.839 --> 00:06:40.320
We did.

00:06:40.959 --> 00:06:44.239
Thank you. Well, your project was a very good…

